{
  "summary": "Clarify and assert input preconditions when hashing signature-cache keys for ECDSA and Schnorr verification. The change extracts the signature-cache hashing logic into a typed helper, returns cache entries by value instead of writing through an output parameter, asserts valid public key encodings and 64-byte Schnorr signatures at entry points, and expands unit and fuzz tests for cached verification.",
  "problem": "Signature-cache hashing serializes variable-length inputs without explicitly encoding lengths, relying on implicit assumptions (such as valid public-key prefixes dictating length and fixed Schnorr sizes) that are not enforced or documented at public entry points. If callers pass malformed or unexpected input structures, the cache key layout could be ambiguous.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author notes the motivation came from the Elements rangeproof-cache key collision vulnerability in the Liquid incident: 'Inspired by the Elements rangeproof-cache vulnerability behind the recent Liquid incident and the subsequent cache-key fix, this change clarifies an easy-to-misread hashing pattern: Write(data, size) hashes the data bytes without encoding the size.' (PR description)"
